The sum of the voltages in a closed loop can be expressed as:
\begin{equation}
\text{Sum of } V = 0
\text{ (Kirchhoff's Voltage Law)}
\tag{KVL}
\text{This means that the algebraic sum of all voltages in a loop must equal zero.}
\text{This includes all voltage rises and drops.}
\text{If } V_1, V_2, \text{ and } V_n \text{ are the voltages in the loop, then:}
\begin{equation}
V_1 + V_2 + ... + V_n = 0
\tag{KVL}
\text{This law is fundamental in circuit analysis.}
\text{It ensures energy conservation in electrical circuits.}

The number of values that can be represented by bits is given by the formula: 

\begin{equation}
N = 2^n
\text{where } n \text{ is the number of bits.}
\begin{equation}

For 4 bits, we have:
\begin{equation}
N = 2^4 = 16
\begin{equation}

Bernoulli's principle states that in a flowing fluid, the sum of the pressure energy, kinetic energy per unit volume, and potential energy per unit volume remains constant. This can be expressed as: 
\begin{equation} P + \frac{1}{2} \rho v^2 + \rho gh = \text{constant} \\ \text{where } P \text{ is pressure, } \rho \text{ is fluid density, } v \text{ is fluid velocity, and } h \text{ is elevation.} 
\text{Thus, it represents the conservation of energy in a flowing fluid.} 
\end{equation}
